{"study_accession":"PRJNA418772","secondary_study_accession":"SRP125117","title":"Antibodies That Convert Bone Marrow Into Trafficking Microglia-Like Cells Reduce Brain Amyloid","description":"One goal of regenerative medicine is to repair damaged tissue. This requires not only generating new cells of the proper phenotype, but also selecting for those that properly integrate into sites of injury. In our laboratory we are using a cell migration based in vivo selection system to generate antibodies that induce cells to both differentiate and selectively localize to different tissues. Here we describe an antibody that induces bone marrow stem cells to differentiate into microglia-like cells that traffic to the brain where they organize into typical networks. Interestingly, in the APP/PS1 Alzheimer's disease mouse model these induced microglia-like cells are found at sites of plaque formation and significantly reduce their number. These results raise the intriguing question as to whether one can use such antibody-induced differentiation of stem cells to essentially recapitulate embryogenesis in adults to discover cells that can regenerate damaged organ systems. Overall design: Total RNAs were isolated in replicates of three from untreated human CD34+ cells, human CD34+ cells treated with B1 Ab, and human CD34+ cells treated with M-CSF.","pubmed":"29295920","geo_series":"","n_samples":9,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN08035067","study_accession":"PRJNA418772","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"study","title":"B1Ab2","description":"B1Ab2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2859355","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2017-12-08","attributes":[["source_name","CD34 positive hemapoietic stem cells"],["cell_type","hemapoietic stem cell"],["treatment","B1 Ab"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6298161","experiment_accession":"SRX3399104","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":23245704,"base_count":1743